WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.897 --> 00:00:02.612
[Music]

00:00:02.612 --> 00:00:06.480
[Michael Feolo]
dbGap is a database of phenotypes and genotypes.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:15.084
This repository houses the data produced in large&nbsp;
scale, usually genomic-based, research studies.

00:00:15.084 --> 00:00:19.140
dbGap has an advanced search&nbsp;
interface. It's solar-faceted.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.868
It allows you to actually&nbsp;
delve into the study details,

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:28.260
the variable details, and even see the&nbsp;
distributions and the values within the variables.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.046
That can all be made public before&nbsp;
you request access.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:37.334
This way, you can browse the data and make&nbsp;
sure that you want to request access.

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:41.520
Some of the variables that we allow&nbsp;
you to search for are individual-level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.680
phenotypic data and potentially&nbsp;
different types of molecular data.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:54.634
We designed a very simple but highly effective&nbsp;
submission scheme where the data sets could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.634 --> 00:01:01.015
defined by the submitter, with the data dictionary&nbsp;
that describes each element within that data set.

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:09.240
We want to try to annotate those variables, that&nbsp;
were collected differently by different studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.520
to some control vocabularies.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.784
And these are largely found in the UMLS which is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.784 --> 00:01:21.240
NLM product. It's a metathesaurus&nbsp;
of several different vocabularies.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.940
And so we've been working with a number of groups.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:30.360
One of the groups is actually from Lister&nbsp;
Hill. They've been mapping the variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.947
when appropriate, to LOINC terms.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.220
And so, we can take in those annotations and&nbsp;
we can make that part of our search interface.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.160
We've worked with a number&nbsp;
of other groups to do this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.760
There's an NHGRI-funded, it's called Phoenix.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.000
It's a controlled set of measures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.100
but those can also be tied back to variables&nbsp;
in a similar way to the UMLS vocabularies.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.565
Another group, based in Heidelberg&nbsp;
Germany, called the Medical Data&nbsp;Models&nbsp;

00:01:57.565 --> 00:02:04.118
are mapping to UMLS CUI terms&nbsp;
and the NHLBI-funded TOPMed project.

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:10.506
With the third party annotations, we're able&nbsp;
to allow people to search across studies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.506 --> 00:02:15.732
find variables within these studies that&nbsp;
were previously more difficult to find.

00:02:15.793 --> 00:02:22.320
We're incorporating these third-party annotations&nbsp;
to include this within our FHIR schema.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.340
So, the FHIR schema is an API to query the&nbsp;
phenotypic and study metadata from the study.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.842
And embedded within the FHIR data structure,&nbsp;
you can put the controlled terms as a system.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.700
I want to get the word out&nbsp;
there that it's a free resource.

00:02:38.795 --> 00:02:41.826
Use dbGap to augment your research.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.993
[Music]

00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:51.187
[Music fades to silence]

